WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.574
In this video you'll learn the orders

2
00:00:03.574 --> 00:00:06.639
in which a transformer's computation can happen,

3
00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:09.681
the one route back down, and how to explore

4
00:00:09.681 --> 00:00:11.993
it all in the tool on this page.

5
00:00:12.455 --> 00:00:15.102
The computation is a causal graph that can be

6
00:00:15.102 --> 00:00:18.736
sliced into time steps in several valid ways.

7
00:00:18.736 --> 00:00:21.581
Information never flows down or left;

8
00:00:21.581 --> 00:00:24.681
the only way back down is a sampled token.

9
00:00:24.681 --> 00:00:26.805
janus, who posts as @repligate,

10
00:00:26.805 --> 00:00:28.698
argues that the architecture permits

11
00:00:28.698 --> 00:00:31.577
introspection on earlier computations;

12
00:00:31.577 --> 00:00:34.876
how much models use it is a separate question.

13
00:00:35.326 --> 00:00:38.275
Take the grid: four positions reading "You

14
00:00:38.275 --> 00:00:41.328
are absolutely correct," and four layers.

15
00:00:41.328 --> 00:00:43.697
janus treats it as a causal graph,

16
00:00:43.697 --> 00:00:46.611
after Stephen Wolfram's physics project.

17
00:00:46.611 --> 00:00:48.271
Every cell is an event,

18
00:00:48.271 --> 00:00:51.313
and arrows show which events need which.

19
00:00:51.313 --> 00:00:53.565
The graph fixes what depends on what,

20
00:00:53.565 --> 00:00:55.551
but not a single clock.

21
00:00:55.551 --> 00:00:58.604
A foliation cuts it into time slices:

22
00:00:58.604 --> 00:01:00.671
steps in which everything an event

23
00:01:00.671 --> 00:01:02.785
needs came in an earlier step.

24
00:01:03.248 --> 00:01:07.207
Reading a known prompt, a GPU goes layer by layer:

25
00:01:07.207 --> 00:01:10.121
every position at once, four steps.

26
00:01:10.121 --> 00:01:12.501
janus draws diagonal slices:

27
00:01:12.501 --> 00:01:15.949
a wave moving up and to the right, seven steps.

28
00:01:15.949 --> 00:01:18.202
Both need the input already written.

29
00:01:18.202 --> 00:01:20.442
Generating goes column by column:

30
00:01:20.442 --> 00:01:22.416
each new position waits for the token

31
00:01:22.416 --> 00:01:24.368
sampled from the one before.

32
00:01:24.831 --> 00:01:26.293
Sixteen steps.

33
00:01:26.293 --> 00:01:29.184
Every legal order gives the same result.

34
00:01:29.184 --> 00:01:30.914
The order is a choice;

35
00:01:30.914 --> 00:01:33.400
what can reach what is not.

36
00:01:33.851 --> 00:01:37.520
Take a result at layer three of position two.

37
00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.923
It can only travel up and to the right,

38
00:01:39.923 --> 00:01:41.525
into higher layers,

39
00:01:41.525 --> 00:01:44.511
never to the lower layers of a later position.

40
00:01:44.973 --> 00:01:46.622
While the model writes,

41
00:01:46.622 --> 00:01:49.722
only one kind of route leads back to the bottom:

42
00:01:49.722 --> 00:01:52.393
the result shapes the probabilities at the top

43
00:01:52.393 --> 00:01:55.295
of its column, one token is sampled,

44
00:01:55.295 --> 00:01:58.128
and it becomes the next position's input.

45
00:01:58.128 --> 00:02:00.067
That channel is narrow:

46
00:02:00.067 --> 00:02:04.165
one token out of about 128,000 carries

47
00:02:04.165 --> 00:02:07.137
at most about seventeen bits, against

48
00:02:07.137 --> 00:02:10.261
thousands of numbers in the residual stream.

49
00:02:10.261 --> 00:02:13.604
That token is the branch point you saw in Loom.

50
00:02:13.604 --> 00:02:17.134
In janus's terms, the KV cache gives no deeper

51
00:02:17.134 --> 00:02:21.127
sequential paths through earlier states, but "a

52
00:02:21.127 --> 00:02:24.773
vast number of parallel computational paths."

53
00:02:24.773 --> 00:02:26.874
Each climbs one layer at a time,

54
00:02:26.874 --> 00:02:30.394
so none can be deeper than the stack is tall.

55
00:02:30.843 --> 00:02:32.770
Her thread follows an exchange

56
00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:34.407
about introspection.

57
00:02:34.407 --> 00:02:35.905
The objection:

58
00:02:35.905 --> 00:02:39.005
a model carries no state between tokens,

59
00:02:39.005 --> 00:02:41.431
so its report of its earlier thinking may

60
00:02:41.431 --> 00:02:43.962
be rebuilt from the words on the page.

61
00:02:43.962 --> 00:02:47.189
Video 2.2 showed that later positions read

62
00:02:47.189 --> 00:02:49.697
earlier keys and values at every layer,

63
00:02:49.697 --> 00:02:53.250
computed from internal states, not just words.

64
00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:56.303
Cached or recomputed, they are the same numbers.

65
00:02:56.303 --> 00:02:59.787
So janus concludes that saying a model cannot,

66
00:02:59.787 --> 00:03:02.805
in principle, introspect on its computations

67
00:03:02.805 --> 00:03:06.242
at earlier tokens is wrong about the architecture.

68
00:03:06.242 --> 00:03:09.493
In her words, "The architecture permits it."

69
00:03:09.493 --> 00:03:11.594
How much models use it, she says,

70
00:03:11.594 --> 00:03:13.603
is "a separate question,"

71
00:03:13.603 --> 00:03:15.599
though she thinks they do.

72
00:03:15.599 --> 00:03:18.212
Research has started to test this:

73
00:03:18.212 --> 00:03:21.764
in an Anthropic paper from October 2025,

74
00:03:21.764 --> 00:03:24.168
Claude models sometimes noticed concepts

75
00:03:24.168 --> 00:03:26.548
injected into their activations,

76
00:03:26.548 --> 00:03:29.986
unreliably and in narrow settings.

77
00:03:30.436 --> 00:03:31.736
All of this can be tried

78
00:03:31.736 --> 00:03:35.068
in the Information Flow Explorer, on this page.

79
00:03:35.068 --> 00:03:36.868
Two highways:

80
00:03:36.868 --> 00:03:39.190
point at a cell to see what it reads,

81
00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:42.824
from below and the left, and what reads it.

82
00:03:42.824 --> 00:03:46.237
Reach: click a cell to wash its past violet

83
00:03:46.237 --> 00:03:48.141
and its future terracotta;

84
00:03:48.141 --> 00:03:51.150
turn on generating to add the one way down.

85
00:03:51.915 --> 00:03:54.562
Paths: step through janus's three

86
00:03:54.562 --> 00:03:56.965
routes from A to B, then scale up

87
00:03:56.965 --> 00:03:58.881
to a real model and compare the count

88
00:03:58.881 --> 00:04:01.552
with the atoms in the observable universe.

89
00:04:02.015 --> 00:04:04.615
Time slices: play the three orders,

90
00:04:04.615 --> 00:04:06.879
then schedule the grid yourself.

91
00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:07.762
A cell that isn't

92
00:04:07.762 --> 00:04:09.818
ready says what it's waiting for.

93
00:04:10.281 --> 00:04:12.046
Inside one cell:

94
00:04:12.046 --> 00:04:15.169
real attention weights from GPT-2 small,

95
00:04:15.169 --> 00:04:16.922
for every layer and head.

96
00:04:16.922 --> 00:04:18.305
Then puzzles:

97
00:04:18.768 --> 00:04:20.567
can this point reach that one?

98
00:04:20.567 --> 00:04:22.343
Every rule in these four videos

99
00:04:22.343 --> 00:04:24.516
can be checked there, by hand.

100
00:04:24.967 --> 00:04:27.010
Keep this. One.

101
00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:29.669
The computation has more than one valid order,

102
00:04:29.669 --> 00:04:33.094
and every order gives the same result. Two.

103
00:04:33.094 --> 00:04:35.544
The only way down is a sampled token,

104
00:04:35.544 --> 00:04:39.003
at most about seventeen bits. Three.

105
00:04:39.003 --> 00:04:42.103
Later positions read earlier internal states,

106
00:04:42.103 --> 00:04:44.228
so janus argues the architecture

107
00:04:44.228 --> 00:04:45.946
permits introspection;

108
00:04:45.946 --> 00:04:49.011
how much models use it is a separate question.

109
00:04:49.011 --> 00:04:51.844
The ideas in this series are janus's;

110
00:04:51.844 --> 00:04:53.481
her thread and the Cyborgism

111
00:04:53.481 --> 00:04:55.828
post are linked on this page.
